00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:18,130
In the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers, centers X thirteen through X eighteen form the Equilateral Triad of Collection One: the First and Second Fermat points X thirteen and X fourteen, the First and Second Isodynamic points X fifteen and X sixteen, and the First and Second Napoleon points X seventeen and X eighteen.

2
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:25,923
Erecting outer and inner equilateral triangles on the sides of triangle A B C produces two families of concurrent cevians.

3
00:00:25,923 --> 00:00:36,800
Connecting the apexes gives the Fermat points X thirteen and X fourteen; connecting the centroids of the equilateral triangles gives the Napoleon points X seventeen and X eighteen.

4
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:53,220
These six equilateral centers are woven into the classical centers by remarkable central lines: X fifteen and X sixteen lie on the Brocard axis with X three and X six; X two, X thirteen, and X seventeen are collinear; and X four, X thirteen, and X eighteen are collinear.
